{:title
 "Section in the Smile Prompt Language, a Thing Wearing a Header and a Footer",
 :description
 "What a section is in THE MAP of Smile, the term for a thing wearing a header and footer wrapper, compared with Markdown and XML, and how my words reached that meaning, from 2023 notes on semantic separation, through the named sections of 2025, to a module that is a section in August 2026, with every source.",
 :keywords
 "prompt sections, Smile sections, header and footer, semantic separation in prompts, response format sections, Markdown vs XML vs Smile, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "In THE MAP, a section is the term for a thing wearing a header and footer wrapper, such as a Smile Section. Markdown gives a header alone, XML a header and a matching footer, and Smile makes both optional in any combination.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP sets Smile's wrappers by the setting in use, and the default carries headers and footers of different hierarchy tiers, so the depth of a section is visible in its faces. Underneath, a section is a position in a [[t:composition]], and THE MAP keeps its parts in [[t:module]], the prefix, start face, label, end face and postfix of each line, and a body with a header, body and footer of its own."
 "Before Smile, in October 2023, I wrote of sections as something prompting already did. On 3 October, a prompt can be illegible “as long as the top and bottom are clearly described as to what it is doing.”[[m:2023-10-03]] On 31 October, “GPT-4 has a limited amt it can do per response, unless you semantically separate out different things into roles/rounds/sections”, and “These are both semantic separators.”[[m:2023-10-31]]"
 "On 3 July 2025, on Discord, I wrote a Smile section out whole, “(: section name (: Content :) END section name :)”.[[m:2025-07-03]] Two days later, “Note that we can stack sections.”[[m:2025-07-05]] The De-GPT document called named sections “the bread and butter of Smile”, and made filling a section its own category, “In Smile, instructions for how to fill out a section are their own semantic category.”[[m:2025-07-08]] Smile v0.3 gave the form, “A section is named on the same line as the smile, and the content is opened into using an open bracket.”[[m:2025-07-09]] In August a section was “a meaningfully different part of the prompt from another part of the prompt.”[[m:2025-08-25]]"
 "In autumn 2025 sections split into those a model reads and those it writes. “So there are sections, which can be prompt sections or response sections.”[[m:2025-10-30]] On 6 November a section's markers each took two faces, “Each start marker has two opens”,[[m:2025-11-06]] and the same day every section became a document, “and it has a header, footer and body.”[[m:2025-11-06#2]] On 13 November I answered a model's comparison, “smile is also a syntax independent of its sections.”[[m:2025-11-13]]"
 "In March 2026 Smile Plan gave the reason for sections, “The way to make the prompt clear is by structuring the instructions into sections.”[[m:2026-03-08]] and in May the Smile Lang document named its top delimiters, “Call section top delimiters section headers because they have smiley heads :-)”.[[m:2026-05-21]] In June the end of a section could carry gratitude, “:) Section Name :) Thank you”.[[m:2026-06-03#2]]"
 "In August 2026 the section became what THE MAP keeps. On 7 August a separator was to find groups inside a composition, “This way we have 5 different levels of hierarchy that always initiate after the header”.[[m:2026-08-07]] On 9 August, “a section is just the Smile default lens.”, “We only have raw text and compositions. and nested compositions”, and “In other words, a module is a section.”[[m:2026-08-09]] On 13 August the whitespace inside sections was to be added by itself, “I don't want any of my prompts failing because of whitespace ever again”.[[m:2026-08-13]] On 15 August an ampersand could string faces together in one header, “(-:&[-:”,[[m:2026-08-15]] and on 16 August, “mouths nose and eyes must match but scarves/hats do not”.[[m:2026-08-16]]"
 "So the section moved from a semantic separator for GPT-4 in 2023, through named sections and response sections in 2025, to the name the Smile default lens gives a thing wearing a header and footer, across {{moments}} moments and {{quotes}} quotes from {{first}} to {{last}}. The words its header carries are the [[t:label]], and the response sections are followed in [[t:semantic-markdown]]."],
 :faq
 [["What is a section in Smile?"
 "The term for a thing wearing a header and footer wrapper. In Smile both are optional in any combination, and the default gives headers and footers of different hierarchy tiers."]
 ["How do Smile sections differ from Markdown and XML?"
 "Markdown gives a section a header alone, and XML a header and a matching footer. Smile lets either be present, and its faces show how deep the section sits."]
 ["What is the difference between a section and a module?"
 "In August 2026 I said a module is a section. Both are names a lens gives to a position in a composition, with header, body and footer."]]}
